A||N||8||BlackDragon||interior paint? & where to get a headliner||||5||
Z||000000||BlackDragon||11-05-2001||12:48 AM||||will interior paint work on the plastic trim peices in my car? i have found every other part of the interior that i need to make it black and red, but i cannot find the inside door trim peices. can i paint them? any advice on how to do it? what kind/brand of paint will last longest?<P>i also need a new headliner, cant find it either, but i have only looked a little, but if anyone knows where to get one i would appreciate the help<P>------------------<BR>civic sucks||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||11-05-2001||12:54 AM||||Take off interior panels to be painted. Any automotive paint supply store sells the interior paint. You can either get it in cans or for a spray gun if you're equiped...easy job. Just make sure you clean everything <B>very</B> well, other wise the paint wont stick.<BR>For the headliner its usually available at fabric stores. Ive redone the headliners in all my Fieros...again, easy job.<BR>After you take off the necc interior pieces, <B>carefully</B> remove the headliner board. Take off the old fabric, get a brush and clean all the crap off the board. Use 3M spray glue (or equivalent) and apply generously to both the board and back of headliner material. Cut as needed. Reverse removal order to reinstall. If sunroof equiped, the plastic "liner" around the opening on the inside is simply tucked back in. 30 minute job for headliner replacement at most (minus glue-drying time). <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My7logo_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WhiteFieroBlur||11-05-2001||01:17 AM||||I recently finished my whole door panel. I went to the auto paint store and got vinyl paint. It cost around 7.50 bucks. I went with the most expensive than the 3 bucks ones. It says its special made for curved surfaces and I heard that this is a good thing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Well I prepped the vinyl of the door with acetone very well. And you can tell if you miss some spots cause when you paint, it will be small holes in it. So make sure you clean every inch. <P> I let it dry and I put on my spray paint. It really comes out good. I an no paint expert but you really can't mess up unless your coats just look so heavy in different spots. But as long as its tottaly clean it will come out look wonderfull.<P> For added I took off the carpet to add Vinyl, I should say this is a very fun process. Well not really, very sticky. I didn't know how to apply the vinyl after many tries so I let the pros to it for 15 bucks.<P> I can say it looks like a professional job that the shop said would be 200 for the doors. Thats nuts.<P> For the plastic parts I experimented with some universal spray paint I got from the auto shop. But I think I am gonna goto that auto paint place and see about some more expensive paint probably made only for plastic. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||avengador1||11-05-2001||07:33 PM||||J.C. Whitney also has the paint available.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||11-05-2001||08:05 PM||||My wife's a hell of a rattle can spray painter.  She painted my entire interior with a few cans of generic black (Rustoleum, I think) paint.  It looks great!  She also stained and polyurethaned my new custom center console.  If I treat her nice, maybe she'll do my rear firewall panels, too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||72vinman||11-05-2001||08:10 PM||||I am in the middle of redoing my interior as well. One tip that I might offer is that you get a can of satin clear coat to finish the job with. I have talked to a lot of people that had some wearing of the paint. The clear coat seems to prolong the life of the color. I have already replaced the lower carpet with a nice black carpet and I am in the process of redoing the top vinyl in black synthetic leather. Looks good so far. <BR>Let us know how yours turns out.||||reg||12
Z||000006||FieroLisa||11-05-2001||08:17 PM||||I did my interior and I'm really happy with it... clean the panels with acetone real well. What I chose to do was order interior paint from like PPG but it was like $33...I just wanted a different color than auto zone would sell  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> But anyway on the center console and arm rests I Had to use the flexible int. paint from auto zone as a base coat cause the PPG stuff isnt meant to flex. I didn't use a clear coat, but then again I have had to do some minor touch ups  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Headliner info is same as above... just buy the fabric at any fabric store.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||11-06-2001||05:42 AM||||The more I read this, the more I'm wondering if you just want to recover your existing headliner, or if you're looking for a new headliner board.  (Keep in mind that you can usually repair your old headliner board unless it's in many, many pieces.)  I think the Fiero Store still sells fiberglass headliner boards.  Just under $200US if I remember correctly.  Just don't let them ship it via FedEx.  (On it's way to Utah as of last night for those that followed my "FedEx Sucks" thread.)||||reg||1
